      "source": [
        "---\n",
        "title: \"Errors & What I learned with this Project: Sampson's Monk\"\n",
        "format: html\n",
        "---"
      ],
      "id": "84266f60"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "1. When I tried to make a static network plot, I initially ran **plot(samplk2, vertex.label..)** without converting it to an igraph object first. \n",
        "\n",
        "**a. Errors:**\n",
        "\n",
        "- A wrong graph in a points-style plot, instead of a network."
      ],
      "id": "80d54c5a"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "- 24 warnings: use warnings() to see!\n",
        "\n",
        "**b. Why it happened:**\n",
        "\n",
        "- samplk2 is a matrix, so calling plot(samplk2) triggers base R’s default plot() method for matrices, which treats the matrix as regular numeric data (often producing a scatter/points-style plot), not a network visualization I wanted.\n",
        "\n",
        "- Since the object was not an igraph graph, R did not use igraph::plot.igraph().\n",
        "\n",
        "- Besides, the warnings occurred because the extra plotting arguments I provided (meant for igraph network plots, like vertex/edge options) were not applicable to base R’s matrix plotting, so R warned that those parameters were being ignored or misinterpreted.\n",
        "\n",
        "**c. How I fixed it:**\n",
        "\n",
        "- I explicitly converted the adjacency matrix into an igraph object first, then plotted the igraph object:\n"
      ],
      "id": "6d1842a9"
    }
  ],
